"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs=globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs||[]).push([[254],{26944(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"reductions","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags/reductions","allTagsPath":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags","count":12,"items":[{"id":"np-completeness/14-3-sat-the-building-block","title":"3-SAT: The Building Block","description":"Why restricting every clause to exactly three literals keeps SAT just as hard, and how the reduction from SAT to 3-SAT actually works step by step.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/3-sat-the-building-block"},{"id":"np-completeness/graph-coloring","title":"Graph Coloring","description":"A map, a box of crayons, and the strangest jump in this whole series: with two colours the problem is easy, with three it is NP-Complete. Here is why one extra colour changes everything.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/graph-coloring"},{"id":"np-completeness/hamiltonian-cycle","title":"Hamiltonian Cycle","description":"Visit every city exactly once and come home. It started as a Victorian puzzle game, and it has a twin about roads instead of cities that is easy. Why one is easy and the other is NP-Complete is the real lesson.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/hamiltonian-cycle"},{"id":"np-completeness/np-completeness-in-minesweeper","title":"NP-Completeness in Minesweeper","description":"Phase 2 ends with a game. Checking whether the numbers on a Minesweeper board even make sense is NP-Complete, you can build logic gates out of mines, and knowing whether a square is safe turns out to be hard in a different way.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/np-completeness-in-minesweeper"},{"id":"np-completeness/np-completeness-in-sudoku","title":"NP-Completeness in Sudoku","description":"In Post 1 I said generalized Sudoku explodes in difficulty and moved on. Now I can actually prove it: an n\xb2 \xd7 n\xb2 Sudoku is NP-Complete, and the proof runs through Latin squares and graph colouring.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/np-completeness-in-sudoku"},{"id":"np-completeness/polynomial-time-reductions","title":"Reductions","description":"If you can solve one problem, can you solve another? Reductions are the tool that lets us answer that question precisely, and they are the key to everything in Phase 2.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/polynomial-time-reductions"},{"id":"np-completeness/subset-sum","title":"Subset Sum","description":"Can some of these numbers add up to exactly the target? I finally prove it\'s NP-Complete, by turning a logic formula into a list of carefully designed numbers where every digit is doing a job.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/subset-sum"},{"id":"np-completeness/the-clique-problem","title":"The Clique Problem","description":"What does finding mutual friends in a social network have to do with Boolean logic? The reduction from 3-SAT to Clique is the bridge, and it is one of the most satisfying constructions in all of NP-Completeness.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/the-clique-problem"},{"id":"np-completeness/the-cook-levin-theorem","title":"The Cook-Levin Theorem","description":"In 1971, Stephen Cook proved that one specific problem sits at the top of the entire NP hierarchy, and that solving it would solve everything else. This is the result that launched the field of NP-Completeness.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/the-cook-levin-theorem"},{"id":"np-completeness/independent-set","title":"The Independent Set Problem","description":"I have used Independent Set as a stepping stone twice without ever proving it is hard. Today I pay that debt, once the lazy way and once the honest way, straight from 3-SAT.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/independent-set"},{"id":"np-completeness/the-traveling-salesman-problem","title":"The Traveling Salesman Problem","description":"Write a number on every road from the last post and ask for the shortest round trip. That one change gives you the most famous hard problem in computer science, and a reduction you can do in your head.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/the-traveling-salesman-problem"},{"id":"np-completeness/vertex-cover","title":"The Vertex Cover Problem","description":"Back in Post 11 I used cameras on roads to explain what a reduction is. I never proved that problem was NP-Complete. Today I finally do, and it takes almost no new work.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/vertex-cover"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);